Given, $R_{1}=100 \Omega, R_{2}=R_{3}=50 \Omega, E=6 \mathrm{ V} ,
$R_{4}=75 \Omega$
As $R_{2}, R_{3} and R_{4}$ are in parallel combination, so their equivalent resistance be
 \begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{R^{\prime}} &=\frac{1}{R_{2}}+\frac{1}{R_{3}}+\frac{1}{R_{4}} \\
&=\frac{1}{50}+\frac{1}{50}+\frac{1}{75}=\frac{3+3+2}{150}=\frac{8}{150}
\end{aligned}
or R^{\prime}= \frac{150}{8}$  \Omega$
Now, $R^{\prime}$ is in series with $R_{1}$, so
$R_{$\text{net } }=R_{1}+R^{\prime}=100+\frac{150}{8}=\frac{950}{8}=118.75 \Omega$

Using colour code for carbon resistor, (For A)
$\begin{array}{ccc}\text{ Orange } & \text{ Blue } & \text{ White } \\ \downarrow & \downarrow & \downarrow \\ 3 & 6 & 9\end{array}
$\therefore \quad R_{A}=36 $\times$10^{9} \Omega$
Similarly, $($ For B)\begin{array}{ccc}\text{ Brown }$& $\text{ Red }$& $\text{ Green }$\\ \downarrow & \downarrow & \downarrow \\ 1 & 2 & 5\end{array}
$\therefore \quad R_{B}=12 \times 10^{5} \Omega\therefore \quad \frac{R_{A}}{R_{B}}=\frac{36 \times 10^{9}}{12 \times 10^{5}}=3 \times 10^{4}\therefore R_{A}: R_{B}=3 \times 10^{4}: 1$

Heat produced in a conductor is given as \begin{array}{ll} & H=i^{2} R t \\
\Rightarrow & H \propto i^{2}
\end{array} where, $i=$ current, $R=$ resistance and $t=$ time.
